Translation patch: <Straus as VartimeMultiscalarMul>::optional_multiscalar_mul (src/backend/serial/scalar_mul/straus.rs)

Change: The `nafs` and `lookup_tables` collections become nested helpers; the `nafs.iter().zip(..)`
loop becomes an index loop.

Why: the Aeneas Lean library models only `Iterator::next`; the provided
methods (`map`, `zip`, `fold`, `filter`, `collect`, `rev`/`skip` on adapters, ...)
are missing (AeneasVerif/aeneas#1057), so the original does not build in Lean.
Aeneas also cannot extract a loop that calls `next()` directly on the
`IntoIter` of a generic `IntoIterator` parameter ("Could not find: type_var_id ..
from ExtractBase.Item"), so the consuming loop lives in a nested helper function
generic over `It: Iterator`, called with `x.into_iter()`.

Behaviour: identical: for `nafs`, the helper calls `next()` until `None` and pushes the mapped item for each
element, in order, which is exactly what `.map(f).collect::<Vec<_>>()` does. For `lookup_tables`, the helper stops at the first `None` element and returns
`None` (the caller returns `None` instead of using `?`), otherwise collects all
mapped items in order, which is exactly what
`.map(|o| o.map(f)).collect::<Option<Vec<_>>>()?` does. (`?` on `Option` is also
not modelled by Aeneas.) Scalars are consumed before points, as before; the `zip` becomes an index loop over `0..min(len_a, len_b)`, visiting the
same pairs in the same order (`zip` stops at the shorter side).

Remove this patch when Aeneas translates the original code: delete the file,
run ./scripts/aeneas-translate.sh and `lake build`, and check both succeed.

